id: pm-gh1706
title: "GH-1706: a stale session-state lock makes pm release clear the claim and then exit 4, --force repeats the refusal, and claim only warns"
description: "GH-1706 (filed 2026-11-11). A stale session-state lock (expired TTL from a crashed agent) makes claim and release disagree, and release reports failure after it has written. On a fresh tracker: pm claim exits 0 with the claim_semantic_attribution_unrecorded warning and records the claim; pm release exits 4 with 'Item session-state lock is stale; rerun with --force when supported for this command' but the claim is already removed; pm release --force exits 4 with the same text; pm health reports stale_lock_count 1 with remedy pm gc --scope locks. Four problems: a partial write reported as a failure, two policies for one condition, a recovery hint naming a flag that cannot succeed, and wording that attributes the lock to an item. Expected: release refuses before any mutation or succeeds with the same attribution warning claim uses; one policy for a stale session-state lock across claim, start, release and close (reclaiming an expired TTL automatically is simplest); the refusal or warning names pm gc --scope locks and the lock path and never recommends an unusable flag. pm-rust mirrors the published behaviour (pm-rust#165)."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[5]: agent-ux,concurrency,"gh:1706",locking,recovery
created_at: "2026-11-12T05:13:38.060Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-13T18:02:28.967Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-13T16:33:48.788Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-13T16:33:48.788Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 120
acceptance_criteria: "release never mutates and then exits non-zero on a stale session-state lock; claim, start, release and close share one policy for a stale session-state lock; The refusal or warning names pm gc --scope locks and the lock path, never an unusable flag, and attributes the lock to session state"
parent: pm-dj98
risk: medium
confidence: high
severity: medium
resolution: "mutateSessionState reclaims a session-state lease once it is past its TTL (force_required_for_stale_lock false, under the stale-cleanup gate), the policy the context usage ledger and workspace transaction locks already use. claim and release share recordAttributionAfterCommit: a failed attribution update after the ownership write returns claim_semantic_attribution_unrecorded or release_semantic_attribution_unrecorded with a suggestion naming the refusal code and pm gc --scope locks, never a non-zero exit. A stale lock kept by policy is refused before any write with stale_lock_requires_override (details lock_id and tracker-relative lock_path, next step pm gc --scope locks) and no longer calls a non-item lock an Item lock. close does not write session state."
expected_result: "release refuses before writing or succeeds with the attribution warning; one stale session-lock policy across claim, start and release; the refusal names pm gc --scope locks and the lock path"
actual_result: On the GH-1706 repro claim and release both exit 0 and the claim is cleared once; a forced attribution failure keeps release at exit 0 with the release warning and suggestion; a stale item lock under force_required_for_stale_lock is refused with stale_lock_requires_override before any write and release --force succeeds; the new spec fails against the old lock policy
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-d70h,related,"2026-11-12T05:13:38.060Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-13T15:22:40.744Z","harness:claude-code","Progress (branch fix/mutation-outcome-honesty): reproduced on the 2026.11.13 build (claim exit 0 with the attribution warning, release exit 4 after clearing the claim, release --force exit 4). Root cause: mutateSessionState acquired its singleton lock with force_required_for_stale_lock hard-coded true and force false, so an expired lease could never be reclaimed by any command, and runRelease awaited the attribution write after the item commit without the warning wrapper claim uses. Fix: the session-state lease is reclaimed past its TTL (the same policy the context usage ledger and workspace transaction locks already use); claim and release share recordAttributionAfterCommit, which returns claim_semantic_attribution_unrecorded or release_semantic_attribution_unrecorded plus a suggestion naming the refusal code and pm gc --scope locks; the stale-lock refusal is now typed stale_lock_requires_override with details lock_id and tracker-relative lock_path and no longer says Item for non-item locks. Close does not write session state, so it has no stale-lock path. Negative control: restoring the old lock policy fails the new stale-session-lock spec."
  "2026-11-13T17:10:57.969Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 1 (Codex gpt-6.1-sol xhigh, Greptile): with the session-state lease reclaimable, a writer stalled past the TTL could overwrite newer state, because the write was not fenced. mutateSessionState now uses acquireLockLease and writes inside lease.fence, so a writer whose lease was replaced refuses with lock_lost (claim and release turn that into their attribution warning). The stale-lock next step ran pm gc against the invocation's own tracker when --pm-path selected another one: the cleanup step now derives the tracker from the lock path, adds --pm-path relative to the working directory when it differs from the resolved tracker, and names a manual removal when the lock root is not a tracker (the merge-driver lock under the git directory). New spec tests/unit/core/session/session-state-fence.spec.ts."
  "2026-11-13T18:02:28.967Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 2 (Codex, CodeRabbit): (1) pm gc keeps a lock whose metadata cannot be read or dated, so the stale-lock refusal now names an owner-verified manual removal for those instead of a gc that changes nothing. (2) refusalCode checks the context shape again, so an error named PmCliError without a context never escapes the post-commit catch or makes isAlreadyClaimedError throw. (3) the attribution suggestion renders pm health and pm gc --scope locks with the caller's tracker selection. claim and release moved their advice assembly into helpers so both stay under the CodeFactor complexity ceiling of 16."
